WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.320
The 2010s saw Hollywood blockbusters making
bank like never before.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.040
It was a decade of Disney dominance and franchise
fever, where pretty much every top-earner

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:13.380
was a sequel to a previous movie — usually
one involving a lightsaber or an infinity

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:13.880
stone.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:16.300
But exactly which movies won out in each year?

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:21.199
Disney kicked off its best-ever decade with
the third film in the franchise that changed

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.320
animation forever: Toy Story 3.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.020
The original Toy Story launched the series,
made Pixar a household name, and became the

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.880
biggest grosser of 1995.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:34.100
Toy Story 2 came out four years later, and
earned even more than its predecessor.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.100
With results like that, a third film seemed
like a no brainer.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:41.040
But it would be a whole 11 years before Pixar
returned to the series.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.120
While Pixar made billions in the interim,
animation fans were still desperate to see

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.380
the "conclusion" to the original Pixar series.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.800
And they got their wish when Toy Story 3 opened
in 2010.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.190
It was worth the wait, too, as the film earned
an amazing 98% Tomatometer score, with a stellar

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:57.760
box office performance to match.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:04.000
It earned $415 domestically and more than
$1 billion worldwide, making Toy Story 3 the

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.160
highest-grossing movie released in 2010.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:09.420
Here's a catch for you, though — it wasn't
actually the movie to earn the most money

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:10.280
in 2010.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:16.640
That honor went to Avatar, which was released
in late 2009 — but made $466 million domestically

00:01:16.649 --> 00:01:17.649
in the year that followed.

00:01:17.649 --> 00:01:21.820
Of course, while Toy Story 3 seemed like the
perfect conclusion to the trilogy, everyone

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:25.009
knows by now that you just can't keep a billion-dollar
franchise down.

00:01:25.009 --> 00:01:30.319
Toy Story 4 was released in 2019, and managed
to outperform its predecessor with a worldwide

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.480
take of $1.07 billion.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.300
"To infinity…"

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.120
"And beyond."

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.560
Harry Potter is big business at the box office.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.590
The franchise, consisting of the seven Harry
Potter films and the two Fantastic Beasts

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:51.219
prequels, has totalled more than $2.7 billion
domestically and nearly $9.2 billion worldwide.

00:01:51.219 --> 00:01:55.469
The high point in the franchise came at the
very end of its first run with 2011's Harry

00:01:55.469 --> 00:01:57.520
Potter and The Deathly Hallows Part II.

00:01:57.749 --> 00:02:01.899
Deathly Hallows Part II was pitched as the
epic conclusion to the eight-film saga that

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:06.299
had begun ten years before with Harry Potter
&amp; The Sorcerer's Stone a movie that had itself

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:08.679
been the highest-grossing movie of 2001.

00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:12.989
Surprisingly, the Harry Potter series wouldn't
hit numbers like that again until this final

00:02:12.989 --> 00:02:13.520
installment.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.640
Anticipation for the movie was at a fever
pitch right before release, so much so that

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:23.230
Deathly Hallows Part II set a then-opening
weekend record of $169 million, on its way

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:28.360
to a total domestic gross of $381 million
and a worldwide gross of $1.3 billion.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:31.840
All in all, it was a good year to be a boy
wizard.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.740
But his reign at the top would be short lived.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.620
It's hard to believe now, but Marvel's grand
experiment of a shared cinematic universe

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.050
once had an uncertain future at the box office.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:46.090
Iron Man exceeded all expectations when it
opened in 2008, taking a B-level comic book

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:52.870
character and giving him a movie that earned
$318 million domestically and $585 million

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:53.860
worldwide.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.780
"Is it better to be feared or respected?

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:59.720
I saw, is it too much to ask for both?"

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.580
However, the ensuing MCU films weren't quite
as successful.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:10.930
The Incredible Hulk bombed in 2008 with $134
million domestic and $263 million worldwide,

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:13.240
only barely managing to make back its own
budget.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:17.560
While Thor and Captain America: The First
Avenger fared better in 2011, none of these

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:20.629
films really screamed "biggest blockbuster
franchise ever."

00:03:20.629 --> 00:03:24.440
If anything, The Avengers felt like it might
be little more than "Iron Man &amp; Friends."

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:26.300
But movie history had other plans.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:31.600
The Avengers set a then-opening weekend record
of $207 million, becoming the first movie

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:38.409
to open with more than $200 million, and soon
ended up with $623 million domestic and $1.5

00:03:38.409 --> 00:03:39.960
billion worldwide total.

00:03:40.129 --> 00:03:43.760
But as amazing as these numbers were, The
Avengers' greatest achievement was giving

00:03:43.769 --> 00:03:47.940
the Marvel Cinematic Universe the kick in
the tights it needed to become a $22.5 billion

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.620
powerhouse.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.160
$22.5 billion and counting, that is.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:57.599
Disney may have dominated the decade, but
in 2013 the biggest hit at the domestic box

00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:00.999
office came from a much smaller film studio:
Lionsgate.

00:04:00.999 --> 00:04:04.860
The studio had previously scored a major coup
when it won the rights to Suzanne Collins'

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.310
wildly popular novel series The Hunger Games.

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:12.219
The first film launched the franchise in a
big way, earning $152 million in its opening

00:04:12.219 --> 00:04:18.370
weekend, on its way to $408 million domestic
and $694 million worldwide.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:24.370
Its sequel, The Hunger Games: Catching Fire,
opened with $158 million, and earned $424

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:30.280
million in the United States and $865 million
worldwide, becoming the highest-grossing film

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.620
of 2013… but only domestically.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:37.800
Beyond the U.S., the big winner of 2013 was
the surprise Disney animated smash hit, Frozen.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:43.949
Frozen opened on a one-theater limited release
in late 2013 with $243,000, but then legged

00:04:43.949 --> 00:04:49.900
its way to $400 million domestic and an incredible
$1.27 billion worldwide.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:54.280
While nobody should ever count out Disney
at the box office, Frozen becoming 2013's

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:58.889
biggest hit worldwide was still surprising,
given it wasn't a sequel, reboot, or remake

00:04:58.889 --> 00:05:02.220
— and wasn't based on a popular pre-existing
property, either.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:06.580
From a wider perspective, 2013 proved that
the global box office was more important than

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:11.080
ever, as the biggest domestic hit didn't actually
become the biggest hit worldwide a trend that

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:12.460
would continue in the years ahead.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:18.060
For decades, audiences' movie-going diets
have been fattened by sequels, reboots, and

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:18.560
remakes.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:23.560
Given this movie-going landscape, American
Sniper was a genuine milestone in 2014.

00:05:23.569 --> 00:05:28.320
It was the first original property since 2011
to top the domestic box office, and the only

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.700
movie this century to do so that wasn't a
big-budget, CG-infused blockbuster.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:36.620
This achievement is all the more impressive
considering the movie was also given an R

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:37.120
rating.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:42.480
That said, American Sniper's status as "2014's
biggest movie" is a bit of a misnomer, since

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:44.840
it actually made most of its money in 2015.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:49.700
The movie first opened in limited release
in December 2014, making a little more than

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:53.090
$633,000 in just four theaters.

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:59.130
It opened wide in January 2015 with an incredible
$92 million, and continued to leg it out to

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:01.670
a $350 million domestic haul.

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:05.840
And while American Sniper was the number one
hit domestically, its total worldwide take

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.240
didn't even secure it a spot in the top ten.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.820
The year's biggest worldwide hit was, unsurprisingly,
a franchise picture Transformers: Age of Extinction.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:19.480
The fourth film in the Transformers series
made a so-so $245 million domestically, but

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:23.710
it was big business overseas, with a total
worldwide take of just a little more than

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:25.170
$1.1 billion.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:29.210
American Sniper may have been an historic
achievement stateside, but worldwide, Chris

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:30.600
Kyle couldn't top the bots.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.340
Disney purchased LucasFilm in 2012 for $4
billion, and they didn't do it just to re-release

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:38.420
the Star Wars Holiday Special on Blu-ray.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:42.680
No, the Mouse House was on a serious mission:
to resurrect the beloved franchise from a

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:44.380
galaxy far, far away.

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:49.380
Inevitably, Star Wars: The Force Awakens became
one of the most anticipated films of the century.

00:06:49.389 --> 00:06:53.530
Fans had waited decades to see what happened
to Han, Luke, Leia, and the gang after the

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:56.700
Empire had fallen in 1983's Return of the
Jedi.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:00.580
This would be the first Star Wars film in
10 years too, as well as the first that wasn't

00:07:00.599 --> 00:07:02.250
a prequel in 32 years.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:06.080
It'd also be the first ever that wasn't led
by series creator George Lucas.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:09.520
While the original Star Wars helped invent
the summer movie season, this would be the

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.860
first film in the franchise to open during
the holiday season.

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:14.540
This turned out to be a brilliant move.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:20.490
The Force Awakens opened like a summer blockbuster
with a then-record $247 million, but had the

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:21.570
legs of a holiday release.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:26.750
By the end of its run, the movie had earned
$936 million stateside to become not only

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:31.430
the number one domestic release of 2015, but
the then-biggest domestic hit of all time.

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:36.280
The Force was strong overseas, too, as the
film earned just over $2 billion globally,

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:39.040
making it the biggest hit worldwide of 2015.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:45.039
Almost inevitably, Disney won 2016 too, with
the biggest box office hit both domestically

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:47.600
and worldwide though those were different
movies.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:52.389
Captain America: Civil War kicked things off,
opening in the spring of 2016 and pitting

00:07:52.389 --> 00:07:55.500
Cap against Iron Man in a franchise-shaking
fight to the death.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:59.530
Adding Robert Downey Jr.'s Tony Stark to the
movie proved to be a big boon to the film's

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:05.480
box office, as Civil War opened to $179 million,
nearly twice as much as its predecessor, The

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:06.300
Winter Soldier.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:11.539
The movie managed a massive $408 million domestic
gross, and more importantly, made a grand

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:17.060
total of $1.15 billion quickly becoming the
biggest global box office hit of 2016.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:21.840
After Star Wars: The Force Awakens' historic
run during the 2015 holiday season, Disney

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:25.900
wisely chose to release Rogue One: A Star
Wars Story in December 2016.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:30.439
Rogue One was the first Star Wars movie that
wasn't part of the main Skywalker Saga, but

00:08:30.439 --> 00:08:32.669
moviegoers were nonetheless ravenous for more.

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:39.140
The movie opened to $155 million, on its way
to a $532 million domestic take, and just

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:45.020
over $1 billion worldwide, making it the biggest
domestic hit of 2016 and number two worldwide.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:49.040
The battle for box office supremacy was no
longer a fight between rival studios; it was

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:51.320
a fight between rival Disney properties.

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.400
"I've got a bad feeling about this."

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:59.280
Before the year even began, the biggest box
office hit of 2017 was practically a given:

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:00.490
Star Wars: The Last Jedi.

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:04.220
It was just a matter of how much money Rian
Johnson's followup to The Force Awakens would

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:04.720
make.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.940
That said, 2017 featured strong performances
from several other films.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:13.420
Disney's live-action Beauty and the Beast
remake opened in the spring, earning $504

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:17.490
million domestically and $1.26 billion worldwide.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:22.880
The Fate of the Furious opened in the summer,
earning a respectable $226 million domestically

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:27.750
but making an insane $1.2 billion worldwide,
becoming the year's biggest international

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:29.910
hit by a pretty sizable margin.

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:34.180
You can't stop destiny though, and Star Wars
was destined to dominate.

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:34.980
"Not now [...]"

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:44.240
The Last Jedi opened during the 2017 holiday
season with $220 million less than its predecessor,

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:48.220
but that wasn't too surprising given it wasn't
preceded by decades of pent-up demand.

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:52.560
It's possible that a divided response from
moviegoers and a stronger-than-expected performance

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:55.640
from Jumanji: Welcome to the Jungle didn't
exactly help, either.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:02.140
But the film made $620 million domestically
and $1.3 billion worldwide, making it easily

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:03.399
the biggest hit of the year.

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:04.700
And that's not so bad, is it?

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:10.160
The only Star Wars movie released in 2018 somehow managed to tank at the box office,

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:14.340
so it soon became pretty obvious that a Marvel
movie would take the year's top spot.

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:18.019
However, the Marvel movie that won the day
domestically was a bit of a surprise.

00:10:18.019 --> 00:10:22.610
Black Panther opened in February, a season
not typically known for blockbuster releases,

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:27.800
and rode a wave of awesome reviews and positive
sentiment to score a mammoth $202 million

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:29.220
on its opening weekend.

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:33.480
Black Panther wasn't a one-weekend wonder
either, as it continued to leg it out, earning

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:38.550
a historic $700 million domestically and $1.3
billion worldwide.

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:43.269
Black Panther was the biggest domestic hit
of 2018 and is one of only a handful of movies

00:10:43.269 --> 00:10:47.210
ever to earn more than $700 million at the
domestic box office.

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:51.160
But while that movie was an historic surprise,
the biggest worldwide earner was a given.

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:55.220
Avengers: Infinity War was the beginning of
the sort-of-end for the most successful franchise

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:59.769
of all time, bringing together a range of
characters from across the entire MCU.

00:10:59.769 --> 00:11:04.220
With anticipation at a fever pitch, Infinity
War set the then-opening weekend record with

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:05.959
$257 million.

00:11:05.959 --> 00:11:10.130
The movie couldn't quite match Black Panther's
runaway success at the domestic box office,

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:13.810
though it still earned a mammoth $678 million
stateside.

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:18.650
And with a global take of just over $2 billion,
it was the biggest hit of the year worldwide

00:11:18.650 --> 00:11:19.420
by far.

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:24.260
After Infinity War's shocking cliffhanger
ending, Disney made a shrewd decision with

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:25.880
the marketing for the movie's follow-up.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:29.959
Fans didn't even know the film's title until
a few months before its release, and the trailers

00:11:29.959 --> 00:11:34.010
revealed practically nothing about the story
only that the story's remaining heroes would

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:36.080
do "whatever it takes" to stop Thanos.

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:40.220
"I am inevitable."

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:43.300
This mysterious marketing campaign paid off
in spades.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:47.760
While it was set in stone that Avengers: Endgame
would break records, not even the most optimistic

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:51.380
observers could predict that the movie would
obliterate the opening weekend record set

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:58.380
by its predecessor, earning $357 million domestically,
and more than $1.2 billion worldwide.

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:04.260
A midsummer expansion propelled Endgame's
final gross to $858 million domestically and

00:12:04.261 --> 00:12:07.709
an incredible $2.79 billion worldwide.

00:12:07.709 --> 00:12:12.750
Its combined total makes it not only the biggest
hit of 2019 stateside and worldwide, but the

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:15.060
highest-grossing film of all time, too.

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:17.360
